      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "A 22-year-old Malay soldier developed dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome 12 weeks after taking maloprim (dapsone 100 mg/pyrimethamine 12.5 mg) for anti-malarial prophylaxis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL PICTURE",
          "content": "He presented with fever, rash, lymphadenopathy and multiple-organ involvement including serositis, hepatitis and thyroiditis. Subsequently, he developed congestive heart failure with a reduction in ejection fraction on echocardiogram, and serum cardiac enzyme elevation consistent with a hypersensitivity myocarditis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "TREATMENT",
          "content": "Maloprim was discontinued and he was treated with steroids, diuretics and an angiotensin-converting-enzyme inhibitor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME",
          "content": "He has made a complete recovery with resolution of thyroiditis and a return to normal ejection fraction 10 months after admission."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In summary, we report a case of dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome with classical symptoms of fever, rash and multi-organ involvement including a rare manifestation of myocarditis. To our knowledge, this is the first case of dapsone-related hypersensitivity myocarditis not diagnosed in a post-mortem setting. As maloprim is widely used for malaria prophylaxis, clinicians need to be aware of this unusual but potentially serious association."
        }
      ]},
